al Trials\nClinical Trials Overview\nCurrent Trials\nOwners\nInvestigator Support\nSponsors\nVeterinarians (rDVMs)\nSupport the BBVCTO\nReferring Veterinarians\nResources\nAbout\nBack to Main Menu\nAbout\nAbout Overview\nCareers\nCollege of Veterinary Medicine\nLocations\nGiving\nEmergency\nI am a...\nToggles the 'I am a' Menu with links to the OSU Veterinary Medical Center and the College of Veterinary Medicine\nI am a...\nToggles the 'I am a' Menu with links to the OSU Veterinary Medical Center and the College of Veterinary Medicine\nVeterinary Medical Center\nCompanion Animal Owner\nCompanion Animal Services\nHummel & Trueman Hospital for Companion Animals\nFrank Stanton Veterinary Spectrum of Care Clinic\nVeterinary Medical Center at Dublin\nClinical Trials\nFarm Animal or Horse Owner\nFarm Animal Services\nHospital for Farm Animal\nLarge Animal Services at Marysville\nEquine Services\nGalbreath Equine Center\nReferring Veterinarian\nReferring Veterinarian Portal\nPartnering With You\nClinical Trials\nJob Seeker\nCareers\nAbout the VMC\nContact Us\nCollege of Veterinary Medicine\nProspective Student\nAcademic Programs\nFinancial Aid\nBuckeye Vet Camp\nMiddle School and High School Prep\nResidency Programs\nCurrent Student\nFinancial Aid\nClass Schedule\nAcademic Programs\nVeterinary Student Research Opportunities\nCollege Research Day\nHealth and Wellbeing Resources\nAlumni or Friend\nAlumni Section\nAlumni Society\nGet Involved\nGiving\nContinuing Education\nResearcher\nSignature Programs\nGrant Support\nEnvironmental Health and Safety\nVeterinary Bioscience Services\nFaculty or Staff Member\nHR.OSU.EDU\nCVM Community\nHealth and Wellbeing Resources\nVIS - Veterinary Information Systems\nWorkday\nCompanion Animal Owner\nBack\nCompanion Animal Owner\nCompanion Animal Services\nHummel & Trueman Hospital for Companion Animals\nFrank Stanton Veterinary Spectrum of Care Clinic\nVeterinary Medical Center at Dublin\nClinical Trials\nFarm Animal or Horse Owner\nBack\nFarm Animal or Horse Owner\nFarm Animal Services\nHospital for Farm Animal\nLarge Animal Services at Marysville\nEquine Services\nGalbreath Equine Center\nReferring Veterinarian\nBack\nReferring Veterinarian\nReferring Veterinarian Portal\nPartnering With You\nClinical Trials\nJob Seeker\nBack\nJob Seeker\nCareers\nAbout the VMC\nContact Us\nBuckeyeLink\nFind People\nWebmail\nSearch Ohio State\nClose Emergency Menu\nNeed immediate care?\nCompanion\nEmergency\n(614) 889-8070\nVeterinary Medical Center at Dublin\nAfter-hours urgent care\nEquine\nEmergency\n(614) 292-6661\nGalbreath Equine Center & Ralph Rogan Equine Performance Evaluation Arena\n24 hours / 7 days a week\nFarm\nEmergency\n(614) 292-6661\nHospital for Farm Animals\n24 hours / 7 days a week\nCompanion\nEmergency\n(614) 292-3551\nHummel Trueman Hospital for Companion Animals\nOpen 24 hours\nEquine\nFarm\nEmergency\n(937) 642-2936\nLarge Animal Services at Marysville\n24 hours / 7 days a week\nLearn more about all of our emergency, urgent, and critical care services\nArtificial intelligence-based image analysis of lymph node mast cells in canine cutaneous mast cell tumors\nThis will assess a new approach to analyze metastasis of cutaneous mast cell tumors, a common skin cancer in dogs. Researchers hope to find patterns that can help predict how aggressive the cancer is and how it might progress. The goal of this research is to identify clearer signs of aggressive tumors and possibly inform clinicians as to how to approach treating this cancer.\nDogs must have a fine needle aspirate (cytology) of the tumor, confirming the diagnosis of cutaneous mast cell tumor\nDogs in this study undergo radiographic indirect lymphangiography, which is a technique that allows clinicians to locate lymph nodes that the cancer is considered most likely to metastasize to. This procedure involves the injection of a contrast medium that can be visualized on radiographs (x-rays).  Any lymph nodes identified by this process can be removed and assessed for cancer metastasis.\nThis procedure represents a clinically standard means of identifying possible lymph nodes for removal and evaluation, and therefore, may be recommended in animals even if they are not enrolled in this study.\nClient Compensation:\nThe lymphangiography procedure, as well as histopathology/biopsy fees, will be recompensated.\nPotential Medical Benefits:\nThe data from this study may result in a novel nodal grading scheme that will help predict biological behavior of MCTs. In addition, nodal extirpation/removal has been shown to be associated with an overall better prognosis.\nPotential Medical Risks:\nThere are some risks associated with anesthesia and surgery that will be explained by your clinician and detailed on the consent form for the procedure. There are some risks, though uncommon, associated with the administration of the contrast medium and any possible sedation necessary for the lymphangiography. While it is possible that your dog may experience unexpected side effects from the sedation or contrast medium, your dog will be observed closely for any side effects and appropriate medical care provided if necessary.\nWhat qualifies my pet for enrollment?\nInclusion Criteria:\n•    Cytologic diagnosis of 1-2 cutaneous MCT\n•    Amenable to surgical excision\n•    At least 7-day washout of corticosteroids, if administered\n•    Surgery within ~2 weeks of nodal staging\nExclusion Criteria:\n•    Recurrent MCT\n•    Cytologic evidence of visceral metastasis\n•    Concurrent systemic illness\n•    >2 MCTs in the same nodal drainage basin\n•    Received previous neoadjuvant therapy or radiotherapy (steroids (with washout noted above) and antihistamines acceptable)\nDiagnosis/Condition Being Studied:\nMast cell tumor\nDownload PDF\nPRE-SCREENING QUESTIONNAIRE\nIf you believe your pet may be eligible for this study, please complete a pre-screening questionnaire.\nPre-Screen Here\nPre-Screen Here\nContacts:\nDr. Ryan Jennings:\njennings.398@osu.edu\nDr. Laura Selmic:\nselmic.1@osu.edu\nThe Ohio State University\nVeterinary Medical Center Facebook Page\nVeterinary Medical Center Instagram Page\nVeterinary Medical Center Twitter Page\nVeterinary Medical Center LinkedIn Page\nVeterinary Medical Center YouTube Page\nVeterinary Medical Center at Dublin\n5020 Bradenton Avenue\nDublin 43017 OH\n(614) 889-8070\nFrank Stanton Veterinary Spectrum of Care Clinic\n655 Vernon L. Tharp St\nColumbus 43210 OH\n(614) 292-1573\nGalbreath Equine Center & Ralph Rogan Equine Performance Evaluation Arena\n685 Vernon L. Tharp Street\nColumbus 43210 OH\n(614) 292-6661\nHospital for Farm Animals\n601 Vernon L. Tharp St\nColumbus 43210 OH\n(614) 292-6661\nHummel Trueman Hospital for Companion Animals\n601 Vernon L. Tharp Street\nColumbus 43210 OH\n(614) 292-3551\nLarge Animal Services at Marysville\n16410 County Home Rd\nMarysville 43040 OH\n(937) 642-2936\nHelpful Links\nCareers\nContact Us\nPay My Bill\nReferring Vets Portal\nVeterinary Medical Center Facebook Page\nVeterinary Medical Center Instagram Page\nVeterinary Medical Center Twitter Page\nVeterinary Medical Center LinkedIn Page\nVeterinary Medical Center YouTube Page\nLooking for the College of Veterinary Medicine?\nPlease Visit\nvet.osu.edu\nPlease Call\n(614) 292-1171\nNon-discrimination Notice\nPrivacy Statement\nCookie Settings\n©\n2026\nThe Ohio State University\nIf you have a disability and experience difficulty accessing this content, contact\nCVM-DigitalAccessibility@osu.edu",
